 "name": "Forest Visualisations Q-Sort (Munich)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the Forstenrieder Park picture interview in Lupp, Kantelberg, Fäth, Hirschbeck, Käsbauer, Ritter, Schisslbauer & Pauleit (2022), Forests 13:1584. Participants sort 16 hand-painted visualisations of winter forest stands near Munich, from even-aged spruce to mixed, layered stands with deadwood, using the paper's forced Q-sort: they choose the 4 pictures they like most and the 4 they like least, then the best of the liked and the worst of the least liked, and say briefly why. Scores: best +2, other liked +1, not chosen 0, other least liked −1, worst −2. Analysis: mean score per picture from −2 to +2 (paper: even-aged old spruce about −0.7, uneven-aged mixed stands with deadwood about +0.6), and the reasons coded by theme.\n\nPictures: the 16 paintings by Anna Ritter in the paper's Figure 3 (CC BY 4.0), numbered 1–16 in the figure's reading order, used with credit; the template is removed if the authors object.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) online instead of face-to-face interviews with visitors at forest entrances; (2) the pictures appear in random order on screen instead of as printed cards; (3) the paper does not list its demographic and forest-visit questions; the four questions here (gender, age, education, forest visits) were written for this template; (4) the paper's second picture survey (12 autumn photographs in Ebersberger Forst) and the Photovoice walks are not included.",
